{Sentence 1: The color gamut of a display is defined as the range of colors it can produce.}
{Sentence 2: Color gamut is a three-dimensional concept, representing hue, saturation, and brightness.}
{Sentence 3: Commonly seen color gamut standards include NTSC, sRGB, AdobeRGB, DCI-P3, and AppleRGB.}
{Sentence 4: sRGB is the standard color gamut established by the International Electrotechnical Commission (IEC) in 1999.}
{Sentence 5: To achieve color fidelity or color management, devices must accurately reproduce the sRGB color gamut.}

{Sentence}
The color gamut is defined as the range of colors that a particular device can produce or record.

{Sentence}
It is usually shown by an enclosed area of the primary colors of the device on the chromaticity diagram.

{Sentence}
A chromaticity diagram with different color gamuts is shown in Figure 1.

{Sentence}
The most commonly known color gamut might be "NTSC" (established in 1953 by the US FCC), however, it is not a standard color gamut.

{Sentence}
"sRGB" is the standard color gamut established by the IEC in 1999.

{Sentence 1: Color gamut is defined as the range of colors which a particular device can produce or record.}
{Sentence 2: Color gamut is usually shown by an enclosed area of the primary colors of the device on the chromaticity diagram.}
{Sentence 3: The most commonly known color gamut might be “NTSC” (established in 1953 by the US FCC), however, it is not a standard color gamut.}
{Sentence 4: “sRGB” is the standard color gamut established by the IEC in 1999.}
{Sentence 5: If all color-utilizing devices, including digital cameras, scanners, monitors, printers, and projectors, can reproduce the sRGB color gamut correctly, then images can be reproduced very consistently across devices.}

{Sentence 1: "The color gamut of a device refers to the range of colors that it can produce or record."}
{Sentence 2: "It is typically shown as an enclosed area on a chromaticity diagram, with the primary colors of the device at its corners."}
{Sentence 3: "Color gamut is a crucial aspect of color reproduction, as it affects the accuracy and consistency of colors across different devices."}
{Sentence 4: "Standardized color gamuts like sRGB help ensure consistent color representation across various devices, promoting color fidelity and management."}
{Sentence 5: "Different color gamuts have different applications, such as AdobeRGB for photography and printing, and DCI-P3 for digital cinema and video production."}
